i## Introduction	/	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
iI've visited many webrings available on the Internet, the	/	tilde.team	70
ihacker webring, xxiivv, noai, geekring, etc. These are all	/	tilde.team	70
iwebrings designed for HTTP(S) only, but these days, I visit	/	tilde.team	70
ia lot of Gopher websites. So I wanted to write a Gopher	/	tilde.team	70
iapplication to implement a Gopher webring. To meet my needs,	/	tilde.team	70
iI created the ange project, pronounced \ɑ̃ʒ\, which	/	tilde.team	70
icorresponds to the word angel.	/	tilde.team	70
hthe hacker webring	URL:https://ring.acab.dev/	ring.acab.dev	443
hxxiivv	URL:https://webring.xxiivv.com	webring.xxiivv.com	443
hnoai	URL:https://baccyflap.com/noai	baccyflap.com	443
hgeekring	URL:https://geekring.net/	geekring.net	443
hange	URL:https://github.com/theobori/ange	github.com	443
i	/	tilde.team	70
i## What is a webring	/	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
iA webring is a chained list in which each node contains	/	tilde.team	70
iinformation about a website and its neighbors in the list.	/	tilde.team	70
iBelow is a graph representing a webring example.	/	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
i/webring.png	/	tilde.team	70
I/webring.png	/~nagi/webring.png	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
i## My goal	/	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
iMy goal is to build a Gopher application that can manage a	/	tilde.team	70
ipersistent webring. Users should be able to submit a request	/	tilde.team	70
ito join, and there should also be an admin section to review	/	tilde.team	70
iuser requests.	/	tilde.team	70
i	/	tilde.team	70
